    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016-sch-1-sec-319-ss-6",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "319(6) Stay of proceedings",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016#sch-1-sec-319-ss-6"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016.pdf, p.8",
      "values:originalText": "(6) In considering whether a stay of the POCA proceedings is in the\ninterests of justice, the court must have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) that the POCA proceedings, and any criminal proceedings of\na kind referred to in paragraph (2)(a) or (b), should proceed\nas expeditiously as possible;\n\nSchedule 1  Proceeds of crime\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Proceeds of Crime and Other\nMeasures) Act 2016\nNo. 15, 2016\n\n(b) the cost and inconvenience to the Commonwealth of\nretaining property to which the POCA proceeding relates and\nbeing unable to expeditiously realise its proceeds;\n\n(c) the risk of a *proceeds of crime authority suffering any\nprejudice (whether general or specific) in relation to the\nconduct of the POCA proceedings if the proceedings were\nstayed;\n\n(d) whether any prejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of\ncrime authority) would suffer if the POCA proceedings were\nnot stayed may be addressed by the court by means other\nthan a stay of the proceedings;\n\n(e) any orders (other than an order for the stay of the POCA\nproceedings) that the court could make to address any\nprejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of crime\nauthority) would suffer if the proceedings were not stayed.\nNote:\nExamples of orders the court could make to address any prejudice that\na person (other than a proceeds of crime authority) would suffer if the\nPOCA proceedings were not stayed include an order under\nsection 319A (closed court) or an order prohibiting the disclosure of\ninformation.",
      "skos:definition": "(6) In considering whether a stay of the POCA proceedings is in the\ninterests of justice, the court must have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) that the POCA proceedings, and any criminal proceedings of\na kind referred to in paragraph (2)(a) or (b), should proceed\nas expeditiously as possible;\n\nSchedule 1  Proceeds of crime\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Proceeds of Crime and Other\nMeasures) Act 2016\nNo. 15, 2016\n\n(b) the cost and inconvenience to the Commonwealth of\nretaining property to which the POCA proceeding relates and\nbeing unable to expeditiously realise its proceeds;\n\n(c) the risk of a *proceeds of crime authority suffering any\nprejudice (whether general or specific) in relation to the\nconduct of the POCA proceedings if the proceedings were\nstayed;\n\n(d) whether any prejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of\ncrime authority) would suffer if the POCA proceedings were\nnot stayed may be addressed by the court by means other\nthan a stay of the proceedings;\n\n(e) any orders (other than an order for the stay of the POCA\nproceedings) that the court could make to address any\nprejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of crime\nauthority) would suffer if the proceedings were not stayed.\nNote:\nExamples of orders the court could make to address any prejudice that\na person (other than a proceeds of crime authority) would suffer if the\nPOCA proceedings were not stayed include an order under\nsection 319A (closed court) or an order prohibiting the disclosure of\ninformation.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016-sch-1-sec-319"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016-sch-1-sec-319-ss-6-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016-sch-1-sec-319-ss-6-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016-sch-1-sec-319-ss-6-logic-descriptionlogic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4d50c376216fbddfd5ec77f9350c3b5884d62d5d0b029cac6802d05dd0368328",
      "skos:note": "Court must consider certain matters when deciding whether to grant a stay of proceedings."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-proceeds-of-crime-and-other-measures-act-2016#sch-1-sec-319-ss-6",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "319(6) Stay of proceedings",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 8,
      "values:originalText": "(6) In considering whether a stay of the POCA proceedings is in the\ninterests of justice, the court must have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) that the POCA proceedings, and any criminal proceedings of\na kind referred to in paragraph (2)(a) or (b), should proceed\nas expeditiously as possible;\n\nSchedule 1  Proceeds of crime\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Proceeds of Crime and Other\nMeasures) Act 2016\nNo. 15, 2016\n\n(b) the cost and inconvenience to the Commonwealth of\nretaining property to which the POCA proceeding relates and\nbeing unable to expeditiously realise its proceeds;\n\n(c) the risk of a *proceeds of crime authority suffering any\nprejudice (whether general or specific) in relation to the\nconduct of the POCA proceedings if the proceedings were\nstayed;\n\n(d) whether any prejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of\ncrime authority) would suffer if the POCA proceedings were\nnot stayed may be addressed by the court by means other\nthan a stay of the proceedings;\n\n(e) any orders (other than an order for the stay of the POCA\nproceedings) that the court could make to address any\nprejudice that a person (other than a proceeds of crime\nauthority) would suffer if the proceedings were not stayed.\nNote:\nExamples of orders the court could make to address any prejudice that\na person (other than a proceeds of crime authority) would suffer if the\nPOCA proceedings were not stayed include an order under\nsection 319A (closed court) or an order prohibiting the disclosure of\ninformation."
    },
